  "poe": {
    "budgetW": 2150,
    "sourceUrl": "https://techspecs.ui.com/unifi/switching/ecs-48-poe",
    "note": "Tech specs print PoE availability as four rows against the (2) 1,200 W hot-swappable modules: 200-240 V AC shared 2,150 W / 100-120 V AC shared 1,750 W / 200-240 V AC redundant 950 W / 100-120 V AC redundant 750 W. 2,150 W is the ceiling - one PSU, or a 120 V feed, or a redundant pair drops it to 1,750 / 950 / 750 W respectively, and the lab must state which. Per-port ceiling is 90 W on all 48 copper ports (802.3bt Type 4, Class 8): 15.4 W PoE / 30 W PoE+ / 60 W PoE++ / 90 W PoE+++."
  },
  "notes": "Ethernet census read from the vendor's own Port Layout table: https://techspecs.ui.com/unifi/switching/ecs-48-poe (HTTP 200, 663,600 B, server-rendered, read 2026-11-20) prints three rows - \"2.5 GbE RJ45 = 16 (All PoE+++) (2.5G/1G/100M/10M)\", \"10 GbE RJ45 = 32 (All PoE+++) (10G/5G/2.5G/1G/100M/10M)\", \"25G SFP28 = 4 (25G/10G/1G)\" - and a Hardware block of \"PoE Ports: 48\", so all 48 copper jacks are PSE and the 4 SFP28 are uplinks. Unlike the 48S and 24S rows this page prints no Console row and no OOB management port (Management: Ethernet only), so 52 jacks is the whole box. Both RJ45 banks were missing the 10M rung the page prints before this correction. PoE: the spec carried no budget, so the loader named it from the model string, found no wattage and served 0 W while the description beside it claimed 2,150 W. Also recorded, not modelled as ports: Max. Power Consumption 250 W excluding PoE / 2,400 W including; Switching Capacity 920 Gbps; non-blocking 460 Gbps; 684 Mpps; (2) AC inputs with (2) 1,200 W hot-swappable modules; (4) hot-swappable fans."
}
